### Which model should you use?

My recommendation is a small, pretrained YOLO detection model, such as YOLO26n in the current Ultralytics model family. A compatible nano model from a previous YOLO generation is also reasonable if it fits the supplied repository or your installed dependencies. The official training workflow supports loading pretrained weights and fine-tuning them on a custom dataset. 【turn116902search3】【turn116902search4】

Why choose a YOLO nano model?

| Requirement         | Why it matters                                               |
| ------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------ |
| Real-time inference | The robot needs new predictions frequently.                  |
| Small model         | Reduces storage and memory requirements.                     |
| Transfer learning   | Reuses visual features learned from existing data.           |
| Custom classes      | Allows the model to focus on cones, barriers and stop signs. |
| Export options      | Makes it easier to test optimized runtimes on edge hardware. |

YOLO is not the only possible model. SSD or MobileNet-based detectors are alternatives, but I would start with one small YOLO model and establish a working baseline before experimenting with additional architectures.

### What is transfer learning?

Training a deep learning model from scratch requires it to learn visual features and object categories from your own data. That takes more training data and computation.

Transfer learning begins with weights learned from a much larger dataset.

A pretrained model already has useful visual features for recognizing edges, shapes, textures and object parts. Fine-tuning adapts those features and its detection head to your target classes.

Your workflow is:

1. Load a pretrained detector.
2. Create a dataset containing your target classes.
3. Fine-tune the pretrained model.
4. Validate it on images it did not train on.
5. Save the best model checkpoint and use it for inference.

One important detail: you generally need to fine-tune the model for your own three-class taxonomy. A generic pretrained detector is not guaranteed to recognize the exact objects and labels your assignment requires.

## 3. Understanding BDD100K — and an important limitation

BDD100K is a large driving dataset designed for computer vision in road scenes. It provides images and labels for tasks including object detection, road segmentation and lane-related perception. The official dataset documentation describes a set of 10 object-detection classes, including `traffic sign`, `traffic light`, vehicles and pedestrians. 【turn116902search0】【turn116902search2】

However, there is a critical catch.

The standard BDD100K object-detection labels do not directly provide all three requested classes as separate classes.

| Your required class | What this means for BDD100K                                                                                                                                                     |
| ------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| Traffic cone        | Not a standard class in its object-detection taxonomy. Cone information is available in its panoptic-segmentation taxonomy, but that is a different task and annotation format. |
| Barrier             | Not a separate standard object-detection class in the listed detection taxonomy.                                                                                                |
| Stop sign           | Covered broadly under `traffic sign`, not necessarily as a separate `stop_sign` detection class.                                                                                |

The dataset's panoptic taxonomy includes a `traffic cone` category, but this should not be confused with a ready-to-use object-detection dataset containing your exact three classes. 【turn116902search0】

### How should you handle this?

Use BDD100K as a source of road-scene images and relevant traffic context, but prepare target-specific annotations for the three required classes.

You have three practical options:

- Annotate a selected subset of BDD100K yourself: Draw boxes around cones, barriers and actual stop signs and label them consistently.
- Combine it with other relevant datasets: Use additional public datasets containing labeled cones or road barriers, ensuring all label formats and class definitions agree.
- Collect and annotate your own images: Capture the objects at different distances, viewing angles, lighting conditions and backgrounds.

For a two-day assignment, a carefully annotated subset is preferable to trying to train on the entire dataset without checking whether the requested labels actually exist.

You can use an annotation tool such as CVAT or Label Studio to create bounding boxes. Make sure every target object in the selected training images is annotated correctly. An unannotated cone in an image can be treated by the training process as background, which may teach the model the wrong thing.

