## 8. Edge optimization: making the model practical for a robot

Training a model is only one part of robotics perception. A model that produces accurate boxes but takes too long to process each frame may not be useful for real-time navigation.

You need to examine the trade-off between accuracy, speed, memory and model size.

### Technique 1: Start with a lightweight model

A nano-sized detector is a sensible baseline because it reduces the computation required per frame.

You can also experiment with input resolution:

| Input size | Likely effect                                                           |
| ---------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| 640 × 640  | More image detail, with greater computational cost.                     |
| 416 × 416  | Lower computational cost, but potentially worse small-object detection. |
| 320 × 320  | Often a useful speed experiment, but may miss distant or small objects. |

These are general trade-offs, not performance measurements for your specific hardware.

For robotics, smaller objects farther away can be critical. Do not select the smallest input just because it runs faster.

### Technique 2: Quantization

Quantization represents model values using lower numerical precision.

For example, a standard FP32 model uses 32-bit floating-point representations. An INT8 model uses 8-bit integer representations for supported quantized computations.

The potential benefits include lower model storage requirements and faster inference on hardware and runtimes that support efficient INT8 execution.

But quantization can reduce detection accuracy. It needs to be evaluated rather than assumed to be better. ONNX Runtime documents static and dynamic quantization; static quantization uses representative calibration data and is generally the more relevant starting point for quantizing CNN-based detectors. 【turn977336search0】

### What quantization changes

FP32

## 32-bit

Baseline precision

INT8

## 8-bit

Reduced precision

Lower numerical precision can reduce storage and improve speed on compatible hardware. Real speed gains and accuracy changes must be measured.

A sensible optimization comparison is:

- Baseline: original pretrained/fine-tuned model in FP32.
- Optimized version: export the same fine-tuned model to a suitable INT8 runtime.
- Evaluate both using the same test images and report model size, FPS, detection accuracy and distance error.

Ultralytics documents export options, including ONNX, OpenVINO and TensorRT, with different supported precision modes and hardware targets. 【turn977336search3】

### Technique 3: Pruning

Pruning removes or reduces the contribution of selected weights or model components.

There are different forms of pruning:

- Unstructured pruning: Removes individual weights, creating sparse weight matrices.
- Structured pruning: Removes channels, filters or other complete structures.

A model with many zero weights does not necessarily run faster on ordinary hardware. The runtime needs to exploit the sparsity, or the architecture needs to be physically reduced, for the expected benefits to materialize.

For a two-day assignment, pruning is optional. Switching to a lightweight model and evaluating quantization are more practical starting points.

### Technique 4: Choose the appropriate inference runtime

Different hardware benefits from different runtimes.

| Hardware                        | A sensible experiment                                                              |
| ------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| Standard CPU                    | PyTorch versus ONNX Runtime                                                        |
| Intel CPU                       | ONNX Runtime versus OpenVINO                                                       |
| NVIDIA GPU                      | PyTorch versus a supported TensorRT engine                                         |
| NVIDIA Jetson                   | A compatible TensorRT deployment, if supported by the device and software versions |
| Resource-constrained ARM device | A lightweight supported model and runtime; measure on the actual device            |

Do not assume that a model optimized for one type of hardware will automatically be fastest on another.

