## 9. How to benchmark FPS correctly

FPS means frames per second.

\\[ \boxed{\text{FPS}=\frac{\text{Number of processed frames}} {\text{Elapsed time in seconds}}} \\]

If a model processes 200 images in 10 seconds, its average throughput is 20 FPS.

That does not mean every image takes exactly 50 milliseconds, but the average throughput is useful for comparing models.

### Use a consistent benchmark procedure

1. Run warm-up inference before starting the timer.
2. Use the same test images and input resolution.
3. Keep the batch size fixed, ideally batch size 1 for the robotics use case.
4. Run enough frames to reduce timing noise.
5. Measure CPU and GPU performance separately on the same machine when both devices are available.
6. Repeat the benchmark for the optimized model.

For a GPU, ensure asynchronous work has completed before stopping the timer. Otherwise, the timing can under-report actual processing time.

