## 10. How will you prove that the distances are accurate?

This deserves its own section because detection accuracy and distance accuracy are different things.

A model can correctly detect a cone while estimating its distance incorrectly.

BDD100K's ordinary object-detection boxes do not provide metric distance for every object, and its annotation format does not guarantee that the camera intrinsic matrix is available for every frame. 【turn116902search0】【turn116902search1】

So you need a distance-validation procedure of your own.

### Create a small, measured test setup

Place cones, barriers and stop signs at known positions. Use a measuring tape or another reliable range measurement to record their true distances from the camera.

Test multiple distances, for example 1 m, 2 m, 3 m and 5 m, where practical. Also vary object position in the frame, orientation and lighting.

For every successfully detected object, record:

- Ground-truth distance, \\(d_i\\).
- Predicted distance, \\(\hat d_i\\).
- Object class.
- Detection confidence.
- Estimation method.
- Whether the detection is correct.

Then calculate distance error.

The absolute error for one sample is:

\\[ e_i=|\hat d_i-d_i| \\]

The mean absolute error (MAE) is:

\\[ \boxed{\text{MAE}=\frac{1}{N}\sum\_{i=1}^{N}|\hat d_i-d_i|} \\]

For example, if the true distance is 3.0 m and the estimate is 3.4 m, the absolute error is 0.4 m.

Relative error can also be useful:

\\[ \text{Relative Error}= \frac{|\hat d_i-d_i|}{d_i}\times100\\% \\]

If the estimate is 3.4 m when the true distance is 3.0 m, the relative error is approximately 13.3%.

You should report errors separately by class if you have sufficient test samples. Otherwise, the overall average might hide a particularly unreliable class.

Important: Evaluate distance error only for detections matched to the correct ground-truth objects. Report missed detections and false positives separately so your distance score does not conceal detection failures.

## 11. Optional extra credit: what the advanced topics actually mean

These are extensions, not requirements for completing the main assignment. I would attempt them only after the detector, geometry and benchmarking pipeline is working.

[Fundamental Technologies Driving the Evolution of Autonomous Driving : Hitachi Review](https://images.openai.com/static-rsc-4/YgsjW7CAz9vAgi5AtYPaKvV1T-Q7l5lTHfSwaFr9uYxXGY2BN3cDui8VtmqHkJrlPOVQ7HSwS-gucNfy7VcruBeKW3pn0jfZ1oh4qePDwLyhdvNKkbMteKV5EEeg1kv0r_QjYS_UupDfPrpgKA3tzbaXlBjMvvD5Fgk7mJ7RPTI?purpose=inline)

A. Epipolar geometry and stereo depth

With two calibrated cameras separated by a known baseline \\(B\\), the same scene point appears at different image positions in the left and right images. The horizontal displacement is called disparity.

\\[ \boxed{Z=\frac{fB}{d}} \\]

Here, \\(f\\) is focal length in pixels, \\(B\\) is the camera baseline in metres and \\(d\\) is disparity in pixels. Unlike single-camera size estimation, stereo geometry can recover metric depth when the calibration, correspondence and baseline are known. It requires suitable stereo hardware or stereo image pairs.

[Advanced Lane Detection for Autonomous Vehicles using Computer Vision techniques | by Raj Uppala | TDS Archive | Medium](https://images.openai.com/static-rsc-4/TcDRTOfHJR6-zV1cD-z2Cwqtq6gODEn8ZsaMAUmg5IY3a2cpn76othsgItDEoxsenbk59PU0l8NNlpB9dd0BrT5L3BFPB0Zo1oPbViTou1b46gLlFvSKFhme5Fh2M3JDnj7INAS7HNeGHwmFnJDYYSFK1ScQTUPyf5ZTSm4Ay_Q?purpose=inline)

B. Homography and bird's-eye view

A homography maps points on a planar surface, such as a flat road, into another view. A bird's-eye transformation can make it easier to visualize where obstacles are relative to the robot's path.

It does not automatically provide the true distance to every object. Its geometric assumptions and camera calibration must be appropriate, particularly when projecting objects that extend above the ground.

[What is the Optical Flow?. Optical flow estimates the movement of… | by Senanur Topal | Medium](https://images.openai.com/static-rsc-4/tkqbjQVrtDyfnXPqMacVQMMsAnevVlFZOmUd7IhpQ1U643CHtvwftmcJJlHiplGLnC3ij0Udr2P5Gy066jFfPPBWPCkbKwDxZc3S5euC0BLHZJ2DZihP5fzekCiPYeXNFUiaZ6r3B4YRUsawwEIk1rKIlcKJLSOLNX2XdAwkReU?purpose=inline)

C. Optical flow and tracking

Optical flow estimates how image pixels move between consecutive frames. It can help follow cones or barriers as the robot moves, smooth noisy detections and identify moving objects.

Tracking can improve temporal consistency, but image motion alone does not automatically yield metric distance. A moving camera, moving objects and changes in camera orientation all affect the interpretation.

For your submission, the main pipeline is enough. It is better to provide one working, measured implementation than three unfinished advanced features.

## 12. Recommended modular code architecture

Keep separate responsibilities in separate files. That makes the project easier to debug, evaluate and explain.

```
robot-navigation/
├── data/
│   └── robot_nav.yaml
├── src/
│   ├── detection/
│   │   └── detector.py
│   ├── geometry/
│   │   ├── camera.py
│   │   └── distance.py
│   ├── inference/
│   │   └── pipeline.py
│   └── visualization/
│       └── annotate.py
├── scripts/
│   ├── train.py
│   ├── evaluate.py
│   └── benchmark.py
├── tests/
│   └── test_distance.py
├── results/
│   ├── sample_detections/
│   └── benchmarks.csv
├── requirements.txt
├── .gitignore
└── README.md
```

What belongs where?

| File               | Responsibility                                                           |
| ------------------ | ------------------------------------------------------------------------ |
| `detector.py`      | Load the model and return detected classes, boxes and confidence scores. |
| `camera.py`        | Store calibration values and camera configuration.                       |
| `distance.py`      | Implement size-based and ground-plane geometry.                          |
| `pipeline.py`      | Connect the camera frame, detector and distance estimator.               |
| `annotate.py`      | Draw boxes, labels and estimated distances.                              |
| `train.py`         | Run the fine-tuning workflow.                                            |
| `evaluate.py`      | Evaluate detection metrics and distance error.                           |
| `benchmark.py`     | Measure FPS and latency for each runtime.                                |
| `test_distance.py` | Check the geometry functions with known numerical inputs.                |

You do not need to build every file as an elaborate framework. Keep the separation useful, rather than adding unnecessary abstraction.

### What should the README explain?

A reviewer should be able to understand and run your project without asking you how it works.

Include the problem statement, dataset sources and annotation limitations, installation commands, training command, inference command, camera calibration procedure, distance-estimation equations, benchmark methodology and actual results.

For distance estimation, document what distance your reported number represents, what assumptions were made, and which method was used for each class.

For model optimization, include before-and-after images from the same test scenes and a table of real performance measurements.

## 13. A realistic two-day implementation plan

The deadline makes prioritization important. Do not try to develop a complete autonomous navigation stack at the same time.

### Day 1

Perception

Step 1 — Inspect the supplied repository

Read its README, requirements, existing implementation and submission instructions. Verify the correct remote repository and expected branch.

Step 2 — Prepare the target dataset

Inspect BDD100K's available labels, collect or annotate images with all three target classes, convert the annotations and create train/validation/test splits.

Step 3 — Fine-tune a lightweight model

Start from a pretrained detector. Train a practical baseline, validate it and save the best checkpoint.

End-of-day goal: A model that detects the required classes in test images, with saved example outputs and meaningful validation metrics.

### Day 2

Geometry & deployment

Step 4 — Implement distance estimation

Calibrate the camera, measure the required physical parameters, implement the appropriate geometric methods and verify them using measured object positions.

Step 5 — Integrate and annotate

Connect detection and range estimation, generate labeled images or video, and handle invalid distances gracefully.

Step 6 — Optimize and benchmark

Measure CPU/GPU performance where available, export or quantize one viable optimized model, and compare its accuracy and latency against the baseline.

