This plan is achievable as a focused minimum viable implementation, provided you can access suitable training hardware and obtain usable annotations quickly. A robust model covering every environment and distance is a larger task, so be clear about the limitations of your data and experiments.

## 14. Common mistakes that could weaken your submission

Before committing, check these points carefully.

| Mistake                                                                       | Better approach                                                                                     |
| ----------------------------------------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| Assuming BDD100K already has all three classes                                | Inspect annotations and create the missing target labels.                                           |
| Reporting camera distance as exact robot-base distance                        | State the distance reference frame, or calibrate the camera-to-robot transform.                     |
| Using an arbitrary focal length                                               | Calibrate the actual camera and use focal length in pixel units.                                    |
| Assuming every object has a standard size                                     | Measure the physical object or use another valid geometric constraint.                              |
| Using the bounding-box bottom as the ground point for every class             | Identify a genuine ground-contact point when using ground-plane geometry.                           |
| Reporting only FPS                                                            | Also report detection accuracy, distance error, model size and test configuration.                  |
| Claiming INT8 is automatically better                                         | Measure its effect on the target hardware and test data.                                            |
| Uploading data, weights or generated files without checking repository policy | Follow the supplied repository's instructions and avoid committing large artifacts unless required. |
| Pushing to your own repository                                                | Verify the designated remote and submit to the repository supplied by Eric Robotics Pune.           |

## 15. What the reviewer is likely to look for

The assignment does not merely ask whether you can install YOLO. It evaluates whether you can connect machine learning with robotics geometry and engineering measurement.

The strongest submission will demonstrate:

Working detection: Your model can identify the three intended classes, with visual output from genuinely held-out images.

Defensible distance estimates: You understand the camera model, state your assumptions, calibrate your parameters and validate the resulting distances against known measurements.

Measured optimization: You compare a baseline model against an optimized version, explaining the trade-off between FPS and accuracy rather than claiming a speed improvement without evidence.

Good engineering practice: Your code is modular, your experiments are reproducible, your README is clear, and the final commits go to the supplied repository.

## My recommendation

Prioritize the work in this order:

1. Get the dataset and class labels right. This is particularly important because BDD100K's standard detection labels do not directly match the assignment's three classes.
2. Build and validate a small YOLO detector.
3. Implement calibrated distance estimation with object-size geometry and, where suitable, ground-plane geometry.
4. Measure distance error using objects at known ranges.
5. Run one meaningful optimization experiment and compare it with the baseline.
6. Document everything and push the result to the designated repository.

You do not need epipolar geometry, optical flow, homography, ROS2 integration and pruning all working to complete the core assignment. Those are extensions. A reliable detector, a mathematically justified distance estimate, real benchmark results and a well-organized repository are the higher priorities.

The most important engineering lesson is this: object recognition tells the robot what is in its field of view; calibrated geometry helps tell it where the object is; and performance optimization determines whether the robot can use that information quickly enough.